From 9eb973ce8afae6563395f5b9dc0c2657fe99fcb3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 03:13:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoShaders][GLES] The 3D LUT of the display in
 Kodi's own video shaders

The shader that writes a picture to the window may now apply the 3D LUT of
the display as the last step of its colour, as Kodi's desktop OpenGL
renderer does with CColorManager: the YUV shader where the picture is
drawn in one pass (YUV2RGBProgressiveShader, YUV2RGBBobShader, and
YUV2RGBFilterShader of GLES 3.1), the scaler of the second pass where it
is drawn in two (ConvolutionFilterShader; the first pass is left as it
is). A shader is given the LUT by its renderer (CLut3dGLES, which holds
its table on the GPU) and only then takes gles_lut3d.frag before main()
and the call of gles_lut3d_body.frag before the dither, after the clamp of
the output: given none, every shader is the one before, to the byte.

gles_lut3d.frag reads the table of dvbridge_lut_atlas.h (an RGB32F
texture on unit 5, read at the centres of its texels) and interpolates it
over the six tetrahedra of a cube, as libplacebo's pl_shader_custom_lut()
does for the bridge, in highp whatever the precision of the shader (a GPU
without highp in a fragment shader does not make it, and the renderer goes
on without the LUT). It takes the signal to full range within [0, 1] (an
output of limited range: 64 to 940 of 1023, as CConvertMatrix makes it),
to the LUT's input range and grid, and its output back; what an output of
limited range carries below its black and above its white goes on beside
the LUT, so that an identity LUT gives that output's codes too. A uniform
tells it not to apply the LUT for a picture drawn elsewhere than into the
window (a capture), as the bridges draw none there.

Measured on Mesa's zink (tools/regress of LibreELEC Hades, stage lutsdr of
dvbridge-render): a picture of an SDR clip drawn the ways Kodi draws one,
with a known LUT in each format, is within 3.1e-7 of the LUT applied to the
picture without it in float64; an identity LUT gives floats within 1.2e-7
of it, and its bytes and 10-bit codes but where those floats are on either
side of a rounding boundary of the GPU (none in four of the seven ways; at
most 23 pixels of 2.1 million in 8 bits and 116 in 10 bits, by one code); a
shader told not to apply it draws the picture without it, to the bit.
